// Let unattended-upgrades install from the Glyndor archive.
//
// Installing a package is not the same as keeping it current: `apt upgrade` is
// something a person runs. unattended-upgrades is what makes it automatic, and
// it only touches origins on this allowlist - which ships covering Debian's own
// security suite and nothing else. Without this line a Glyndor package sits at
// the version it was installed at until somebody remembers it.
//
// That matters most for the products that are exposed: a mail server listening
// on 25, 465, 587 and 993 should not be waiting on someone's memory for a
// security fix.
//
// Numbered above Debian's 50unattended-upgrades so this loads after it. apt
// appends repeated blocks rather than replacing them (measured with
// `apt-config dump`), so whatever the operator already allows is untouched.
//
// To opt out, edit this file - it is a conffile, so dpkg keeps your version
// across upgrades and will ask before overwriting it.
Unattended-Upgrade::Allowed-Origins {
        "Glyndor:stable";
};
